} \approx 361.5. \] Rounding to the nearest whole number gives us approximately 362 msec. So the patient's corrected QT interval is 362 msec. {"step_by_step_thinking": "To calculate the corrected QT interval using the Bazett formula, we use the formula: QTc = QT / sqrt(RR), where QT is the measured QT interval and RR is the R-R interval in seconds. Given the heart rate (HR) is 72 bpm and the QT interval is 330 msec.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the RR interval in seconds. Since the heart rate is 72 bpm, the RR interval is 60 / 72 = 0.8333 seconds.\n\nStep 2: Calculate the square root of the RR interval. sqrt(RR) = sqrt(0.8333) ≈ 0.9129.\n\nStep 3: Apply the Bazett formula to find the corrected QT interval: QTc = 330 / 0.9129 ≈ 361.5 msec.\n\nRounding 361.5 to the nearest whole number gives us 362 msec.", "answer": "362"} The final answer is: {"step_by_step_thinking": "To calculate the corrected QT interval using the Bazett formula, we use the formula: QTc = QT / sqrt(RR), where QT is the measured QT interval and RR is the R-R interval in seconds. Given the heart rate (HR) is 72 bpm and the QT interval is 330 msec.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the RR interval in seconds. Since the heart rate is 72 bpm, the RR interval is 60 / 72 = 0.8333 seconds.\n\nStep 2: Calculate the square root of the RR interval. sqrt(RR) = sqrt(0.8333) ≈ 0.9129.\n\nStep 3: Apply the Bazett formula to find the corrected QT interval: QTc = 330 / 0.9129 ≈ 361.5 msec.\n\nRounding 361.5 to the nearest whole number gives us 362 msec.", "answer": "362"}
