 \): \[ 133 + 3.2 = 136.2 \, \text{mmol/L}. \]\nStep 2: Multiply this sum by 2: \[ 2 \times 136.2 = 272.4. \]\nStep 3: Calculate \(\frac{\text{Glucose}}{18}\): \[ \frac{569}{18} \approx 31.61. \]\nStep 4: Calculate \(\frac{\text{BUN}}{2.8}\): \[ \frac{25.9}{2.8} \approx 9.25. \]\nStep 5: Add all the components together: \[ 272.4 + 31.61 + 9.25. \]\nPerform the addition step by step: \[ 272.4 + 31.61 = 304.01, \] and then \[ 304.01 + 9.25 = 313.26. \]\nRounding 313.26 to the nearest whole number gives us approximately 313 mOsm/kg.", "answer": "313"} {"step_by_step_thinking": "To calculate the serum osmolality, we use the formula: \[ \text{Serum Osmolality} = 2 \times (\text{Na}^+ + \text{K}^+) + \\frac{\text{Glucose}}{18} + \\frac{\text{BUN}}{2.8}. \] Given the values: \n- Corrected sodium (Na+) = 133 mmol/L,\n- Potassium (K+) = 3.2 mmol/L,\n- Glucose = 569 mg/dL,\n- BUN = 25.9 mg/dL.\n\nStep 1: Calculate \( \text{Na}^+ + \text{K}^+ \): \[ 133 + 3.2 = 136.2 \, \text{mmol/L}. \]\nStep 2: Multiply this sum by 2: \[ 2 \times 136.2 = 272.4. \]\nStep 3: Calculate \(\frac{\text{Glucose}}{18}\): \[ \frac{569}{18} \approx 31.61. \]\nStep 4: Calculate \(\frac{\text{BUN}}{2.8}\): \[ \frac{25.9}{2.8} \approx 9.25. \]\nStep 5: Add all the components together: \[ 272.4 + 31.61 + 9.25. \]\nPerform the addition step by step: \[ 272.4 + 31.61 = 304.01, \] and then \[ 304.01 + 9.25 = 313.26. \]\nRounding 313.26 to the nearest whole number gives us approximately 313 mOsm/kg.", "answer": "313"}
